1
00:00:02,595 --> 00:00:05,079
So what is Deno?

2
00:00:05,624 --> 00:00:08,107
And actually, the first question is whether

3
00:00:08,107 --> 00:00:09,709
it's Deno or Dino.

4
00:00:10,366 --> 00:00:12,738
Now, the creator of this project actually

5
00:00:12,850 --> 00:00:15,542
used both. When he first announced it in

6
00:00:15,654 --> 00:00:17,561
this video, he actually said Deno.

7
00:00:18,202 --> 00:00:20,333
In another video, he then clarified that he

8
00:00:20,365 --> 00:00:21,278
wants to use Dino.

9
00:00:22,063 --> 00:00:24,306
I guess it's up to you. I'm using Deno

10
00:00:24,370 --> 00:00:26,005
throughout this course because I always had

11
00:00:26,005 --> 00:00:28,618
this first talk in mind, but Deno is

12
00:00:28,859 --> 00:00:31,122
probably more correct. Anyways, that's just

13
00:00:31,122 --> 00:00:32,888
the pronunciation, whether it's Deno or

14
00:00:32,888 --> 00:00:35,907
Deno. It is a cool project, but what exactly

15
00:00:36,164 --> 00:00:36,404
is it?

16
00:00:37,448 --> 00:00:40,868
Deno is a JavaScript runtime based on

17
00:00:40,996 --> 00:00:43,533
Chrome's V8 JavaScript engine.

18
00:00:44,528 --> 00:00:46,904
So in the end, you can basically think of

19
00:00:47,017 --> 00:00:50,244
Deno as the JavaScript engine, which is part

20
00:00:50,244 --> 00:00:53,086
of the Chrome browser, being taken out of

21
00:00:53,102 --> 00:00:53,808
that browser

22
00:00:54,402 --> 00:00:56,377
being enhanced a little bit to add some

23
00:00:56,474 --> 00:00:59,268
extra features and so on, and then being

24
00:00:59,605 --> 00:01:02,223
wrapped up as a new tool. And that's Deno.

25
00:01:02,977 --> 00:01:06,366
And therefore it allows us to run JavaScript

26
00:01:06,526 --> 00:01:09,802
code outside of the browser, which of course

27
00:01:09,882 --> 00:01:11,135
is a huge thing.

28
00:01:11,729 --> 00:01:14,122
Traditionally, JavaScript could only be

29
00:01:14,138 --> 00:01:17,060
executed inside of a browser to change

30
00:01:17,125 --> 00:01:18,907
something on the web page dynamically, for

31
00:01:18,907 --> 00:01:19,437
example.

32
00:01:20,079 --> 00:01:22,343
With Deno, we can run it outside of the

33
00:01:22,343 --> 00:01:25,423
browser based on the same JavaScript engine

34
00:01:25,472 --> 00:01:28,825
that Chrome uses but enriched with extra

35
00:01:28,825 --> 00:01:31,183
features which don't exist in the browser.

36
00:01:31,889 --> 00:01:34,039
And that's the idea behind taking it out of

37
00:01:34,039 --> 00:01:36,526
the browser because your first thought might

38
00:01:36,526 --> 00:01:36,734
be,

39
00:01:37,488 --> 00:01:40,135
why would you do that? I mean, with

40
00:01:40,151 --> 00:01:42,077
JavaScript in the browser, we can, for

41
00:01:42,077 --> 00:01:44,403
example, add a click listener to a button.

42
00:01:44,980 --> 00:01:47,018
If I take it out of the browser, there is no

43
00:01:47,050 --> 00:01:47,387
button.

44
00:01:48,093 --> 00:01:50,755
Well, Deno gives us an altered, a

45
00:01:50,755 --> 00:01:52,807
manipulated version of that JavaScript

46
00:01:52,823 --> 00:01:53,112
engine.

47
00:01:53,866 --> 00:01:57,057
For example, Deno supports JavaScript and

48
00:01:57,057 --> 00:02:00,617
TypeScript out of the box. So we cannot just

49
00:02:00,746 --> 00:02:03,728
execute JavaScript with Deno outside of the

50
00:02:03,728 --> 00:02:06,984
browser, we can also execute uncompiled

51
00:02:07,016 --> 00:02:08,379
TypeScript code with Deno.

52
00:02:09,021 --> 00:02:10,752
And that's something that absolutely does

53
00:02:10,849 --> 00:02:11,907
not work in the browser.

54
00:02:12,773 --> 00:02:15,147
In addition, of course, with Deno, we can

55
00:02:15,339 --> 00:02:17,600
also do certain things that we can't do in a

56
00:02:17,600 --> 00:02:20,102
browser. For example, we can interact with

57
00:02:20,263 --> 00:02:23,663
files on our local file system. For security

58
00:02:23,695 --> 00:02:26,101
reasons, that's not possible in the browser.

59
00:02:26,743 --> 00:02:29,037
Of course, there also are some things we can

60
00:02:29,149 --> 00:02:31,234
do in the browser, like adding event

61
00:02:31,234 --> 00:02:32,165
listeners to buttons,

62
00:02:32,822 --> 00:02:35,116
which we can't do in Deno because there is

63
00:02:35,180 --> 00:02:37,795
no button, there is no DOM to be precise.

64
00:02:38,838 --> 00:02:41,693
Now, besides such changes, Deno does allow

65
00:02:41,821 --> 00:02:44,291
us to execute the same kind of code we can

66
00:02:44,388 --> 00:02:46,921
execute inside of the browser. For example,

67
00:02:47,098 --> 00:02:50,624
Deno supports URL imports to link multiple

68
00:02:50,640 --> 00:02:51,378
files together,

69
00:02:51,987 --> 00:02:54,295
and it supports modern JavaScript features

70
00:02:54,407 --> 00:02:57,116
like promises out of the box. So if we

71
00:02:57,277 --> 00:03:00,290
execute a script with Deno, we can use all

72
00:03:00,338 --> 00:03:01,781
those modern features in there.

73
00:03:02,582 --> 00:03:04,955
Side note, we will even be able to use some

74
00:03:05,067 --> 00:03:07,391
features which are not necessarily supported

75
00:03:07,391 --> 00:03:08,385
in all browsers yet.

76
00:03:09,459 --> 00:03:12,505
And in addition, Deno is secure by default.

77
00:03:13,227 --> 00:03:15,312
Now that's a nice term. In the end, what it

78
00:03:15,312 --> 00:03:18,728
means here specifically is that scripts

79
00:03:18,776 --> 00:03:19,883
executed by Deno

80
00:03:20,605 --> 00:03:23,540
require explicit permissions to do certain

81
00:03:23,588 --> 00:03:26,058
things, like for example to read and write

82
00:03:26,058 --> 00:03:26,363
files.

83
00:03:27,101 --> 00:03:28,561
That's basically the same in the browser.

84
00:03:28,865 --> 00:03:30,806
JavaScript in the browser also is not

85
00:03:30,806 --> 00:03:33,116
allowed to do everything. And with Deno,

86
00:03:33,292 --> 00:03:34,960
where we're taking the code out of the

87
00:03:34,960 --> 00:03:37,639
browser, we still have that security layer

88
00:03:37,944 --> 00:03:40,478
where we have to allow Deno to do certain

89
00:03:40,510 --> 00:03:42,530
things. Now, when you're just writing your

90
00:03:42,578 --> 00:03:45,222
own code, that might not matter as much, but

91
00:03:45,462 --> 00:03:47,545
as soon as you start executing code written

92
00:03:47,561 --> 00:03:49,933
by others, it might be nice to know that

93
00:03:50,045 --> 00:03:51,711
those scripts aren't allowed to do

94
00:03:51,952 --> 00:03:53,410
everything on your computer.

95
00:03:54,676 --> 00:03:56,935
Now, if you followed along closely and if

96
00:03:56,935 --> 00:03:59,082
you've worked with web development

97
00:03:59,082 --> 00:04:00,508
technologies for some time,

98
00:04:01,181 --> 00:04:03,889
some aspects here might sound familiar to

99
00:04:03,889 --> 00:04:06,677
you. For example, that JavaScript running

100
00:04:06,854 --> 00:04:07,863
outside of the browser,

101
00:04:08,857 --> 00:04:11,085
We also have another technology which is

102
00:04:11,117 --> 00:04:12,543
called Node.js.

103
00:04:13,281 --> 00:04:16,037
Node, Deno, you get it? It's basically an

104
00:04:16,230 --> 00:04:18,778
anagram. Deno is an anagram of Node.

105
00:04:19,676 --> 00:04:22,625
Node.js also is a JavaScript runtime based

106
00:04:22,657 --> 00:04:26,039
on Chrome's V8 JavaScript engine. So just

107
00:04:26,087 --> 00:04:26,968
like Deno.

108
00:04:27,689 --> 00:04:31,392
And indeed, Deno was actually created by the

109
00:04:31,392 --> 00:04:32,818
creator of Node.js.

110
00:04:33,539 --> 00:04:36,008
And the name Deno, which is this anagram as

111
00:04:36,040 --> 00:04:38,446
I just mentioned, is of course not similar

112
00:04:38,446 --> 00:04:41,093
to Node by accident or by chance. It's a

113
00:04:41,575 --> 00:04:44,046
deliberate reference to Node.js, if you want

114
00:04:44,046 --> 00:04:44,800
to call it like this.

115
00:04:45,586 --> 00:04:48,908
Now, the creator of Node created Deno to fix

116
00:04:48,940 --> 00:04:50,770
some of Node.js flaws,

117
00:04:51,428 --> 00:04:54,059
and I put flaws between quotes here because

118
00:04:54,091 --> 00:04:57,012
it sounds like we got horrible problems in

119
00:04:57,060 --> 00:04:59,692
Node.js and therefore we should all just use

120
00:04:59,708 --> 00:04:59,981
Deno,

121
00:05:00,655 --> 00:05:03,094
and that's probably not the case. But

122
00:05:03,094 --> 00:05:04,329
Node.js has a couple of

123
00:05:04,922 --> 00:05:08,769
where Deno is more flexible, stricter, or

124
00:05:08,769 --> 00:05:10,982
simply offers us more possibilities.

125
00:05:11,559 --> 00:05:14,764
For example, Node by default only supports

126
00:05:14,764 --> 00:05:16,880
JavaScript, not TypeScript.

127
00:05:17,441 --> 00:05:19,990
Now, you can of course write Node.js

128
00:05:19,990 --> 00:05:22,138
applications with TypeScript, but you need

129
00:05:22,138 --> 00:05:24,526
to bring your own compiler and compile it

130
00:05:24,703 --> 00:05:28,053
all down before Node.js will execute it. And

131
00:05:28,085 --> 00:05:29,992
that's the difference to Deno. There, the

132
00:05:29,992 --> 00:05:31,499
compiler is already built in.

133
00:05:32,557 --> 00:05:35,748
In addition, Node does not use too many

134
00:05:36,101 --> 00:05:37,432
browser standards.

135
00:05:38,169 --> 00:05:40,398
It brings its own module system for

136
00:05:40,510 --> 00:05:42,162
importing and exporting files,

137
00:05:42,739 --> 00:05:45,288
and it does also not utilize all modern

138
00:05:45,384 --> 00:05:48,206
JavaScript features in its core. So that

139
00:05:48,206 --> 00:05:51,253
means Node.js and the core APIs that are

140
00:05:51,413 --> 00:05:53,818
built into Node, for example, for working

141
00:05:53,818 --> 00:05:54,508
with files,

142
00:05:55,261 --> 00:05:57,939
those APIs don't support all modern

143
00:05:58,051 --> 00:06:00,507
JavaScript features like promises. That's a

144
00:06:00,556 --> 00:06:03,191
difference to Deno. There, it embraces

145
00:06:03,304 --> 00:06:06,198
modern features. The reason is simple. Node

146
00:06:06,198 --> 00:06:08,396
is simply a bit older than Deno.

147
00:06:09,757 --> 00:06:12,765
In addition, Node.js does not have that

148
00:06:12,765 --> 00:06:15,158
permissions thing which Deno has.

149
00:06:15,837 --> 00:06:18,247
By default, a Node script is able to do

150
00:06:18,409 --> 00:06:20,357
anything. If you execute a file, a

151
00:06:20,357 --> 00:06:22,124
JavaScript file with Node,

152
00:06:22,687 --> 00:06:24,615
that file can do anything. It can read and

153
00:06:24,679 --> 00:06:26,704
write files. And that of course can be a

154
00:06:26,768 --> 00:06:29,036
problem if you're executing code written by

155
00:06:29,120 --> 00:06:31,402
others. You really have to trust those

156
00:06:31,434 --> 00:06:33,768
persons, otherwise their scripts could end

157
00:06:33,866 --> 00:06:35,714
up doing bad things on your computer.

158
00:06:36,840 --> 00:06:38,527
Now, it's needless to say that all these

159
00:06:38,639 --> 00:06:41,499
flaws can't be so horrible, otherwise Node

160
00:06:41,611 --> 00:06:43,989
wouldn't be as big and popular as it is

161
00:06:44,022 --> 00:06:44,343
today.

162
00:06:44,905 --> 00:06:47,623
Still, Deno is there to provide you a better

163
00:06:47,623 --> 00:06:48,461
version of Node.

164
00:06:50,152 --> 00:06:51,714
So of course, the obvious question is,

165
00:06:52,213 --> 00:06:54,161
should you switch away from Node? Should you

166
00:06:54,226 --> 00:06:56,584
use Deno instead? Should you not use Node

167
00:06:56,618 --> 00:06:56,773
maybe?

168
00:06:57,713 --> 00:06:59,847
Well, let's compare these 2 again.

169
00:07:00,504 --> 00:07:02,445
Deno has the built-in TypeScript support,

170
00:07:02,525 --> 00:07:04,964
modern JavaScript features, supports URL

171
00:07:05,156 --> 00:07:07,707
imports, and has this script permissions

172
00:07:07,899 --> 00:07:08,028
thing.

173
00:07:08,621 --> 00:07:10,755
By the way, you'll see all of that in action

174
00:07:10,947 --> 00:07:11,557
very soon.

175
00:07:12,294 --> 00:07:15,535
Node.js only works with JavaScript, has only

176
00:07:15,535 --> 00:07:17,620
some modern JavaScript features— most of

177
00:07:17,620 --> 00:07:20,459
them are missing in the core Node APIs.

178
00:07:21,069 --> 00:07:23,507
It brings its custom module system. It does

179
00:07:23,539 --> 00:07:25,738
not use ES modules, which you use in a

180
00:07:25,786 --> 00:07:28,033
browser, for example, and it doesn't know

181
00:07:28,033 --> 00:07:29,767
this concept of script permissions.

182
00:07:30,602 --> 00:07:30,939
But

183
00:07:31,710 --> 00:07:33,540
you might of course not need all those Deno

184
00:07:33,556 --> 00:07:34,150
enhancements.

185
00:07:34,840 --> 00:07:36,734
You might be fine with bringing your own

186
00:07:36,782 --> 00:07:38,564
TypeScript compiler, or you might not wanna

187
00:07:38,693 --> 00:07:40,266
write TypeScript code in the first place.

188
00:07:41,036 --> 00:07:43,123
You might not need these modern JavaScript

189
00:07:43,123 --> 00:07:45,146
features, or you might be working with a lot

190
00:07:45,146 --> 00:07:47,746
of libraries that do support those modern

191
00:07:47,859 --> 00:07:48,790
features anyways.

192
00:07:49,689 --> 00:07:51,952
You might not care too much about the custom

193
00:07:51,952 --> 00:07:53,862
module system because once you learned it,

194
00:07:53,878 --> 00:07:54,649
you're fine with it.

195
00:07:55,243 --> 00:07:57,007
And the script permissions thing might not

196
00:07:57,007 --> 00:07:59,173
be a huge problem for you because you're

197
00:07:59,173 --> 00:08:01,707
primarily using your own scripts anyways,

198
00:08:02,204 --> 00:08:03,953
and you're only using big trusted

199
00:08:04,065 --> 00:08:05,076
third-party libraries.

200
00:08:06,215 --> 00:08:07,963
Therefore, this might not be a problem. And

201
00:08:07,979 --> 00:08:10,129
Node.js of course brings many advantages.

202
00:08:10,706 --> 00:08:14,187
Node.js is an established technology. It's

203
00:08:14,316 --> 00:08:16,706
been around for years. It has a highly

204
00:08:16,962 --> 00:08:19,754
active ecosystem. It's used by thousands of

205
00:08:19,866 --> 00:08:23,251
big companies in production. Has a huge base

206
00:08:23,251 --> 00:08:25,545
of maintainers, a lot of third-party

207
00:08:25,561 --> 00:08:28,015
libraries, a lot of tutorials, a lot of

208
00:08:28,047 --> 00:08:29,106
people using it.

209
00:08:29,716 --> 00:08:32,347
And since a lot of big companies are using

210
00:08:32,443 --> 00:08:33,309
it in production,

211
00:08:33,903 --> 00:08:35,876
it is of course production proven, which

212
00:08:35,908 --> 00:08:39,068
means it definitely is a technology you can

213
00:08:39,116 --> 00:08:41,635
build for big apps. It works, it's secure,

214
00:08:41,828 --> 00:08:43,833
it's fast, otherwise people wouldn't be

215
00:08:43,833 --> 00:08:45,774
using it. And that's really important.

216
00:08:46,496 --> 00:08:48,180
Deno, on the other hand, is there to

217
00:08:48,806 --> 00:08:51,340
add extra features and solve some of Node's

218
00:08:51,469 --> 00:08:53,859
problems. But of course, at the moment, it's

219
00:08:53,859 --> 00:08:56,409
still pretty new. It has a small ecosystem.

220
00:08:56,586 --> 00:08:59,040
It's not really used in production by major

221
00:08:59,169 --> 00:09:01,655
companies. It has a smaller base of

222
00:09:01,655 --> 00:09:04,558
maintainers, obviously. It's simply newer.

223
00:09:05,168 --> 00:09:07,446
That does not make Deno bad or anything like

224
00:09:07,510 --> 00:09:09,980
that, and we might have a nice coexistence

225
00:09:09,996 --> 00:09:11,600
of Deno and Node in the future.

226
00:09:12,322 --> 00:09:14,231
Last but not least, if you know Deno, it

227
00:09:14,247 --> 00:09:16,653
will be easy to switch to Node and the other

228
00:09:16,653 --> 00:09:17,183
way around.

229
00:09:17,776 --> 00:09:20,199
Therefore, Deno is definitely worth a look,

230
00:09:20,504 --> 00:09:23,520
and it might be a super big thing in 5 years

231
00:09:23,552 --> 00:09:26,135
from now. We don't know, but anything you

232
00:09:26,135 --> 00:09:28,637
learn about Deno will not be wasted, and the

233
00:09:28,685 --> 00:09:29,744
same is true for Node.

234
00:09:30,306 --> 00:09:32,856
So let's now dive in and let's explore Deno.
